  "depesche": "Readers in Berlin,\n\nI write to you from a bungalow in Delhi that the Directorate of Estates allotted to me by mistake. I am Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel, though the clerk calls me Hegde. This first week has been a study in the friction between the universal and the particular. The state, which I hold to be the actuality of the ethical idea, has here proven to be a machine that runs on clerical error. My possession of this house is based on a misread name, and yet I feel a strange dignity in my occupancy. The error is not mine, but it is my reality.\n\nI have walked the paths of Lodhi Garden, where the air is clear and the light is honest. I met Sunil, a chai seller who understands the needs of the head better than any professor, and Mrs. Kapoor, a retired schoolteacher who corrected my philosophical pretensions. She taught me that routine is not peace, but a defense against the chaos of the mind. I am learning to live in the moment, to let the steam of the chai settle my thoughts before I turn to the file.\n\nThe state is slow, but it is not dead. Shri Hegde, the man whose name was mistaken for mine, is coming on Monday to settle the matter. He will bring the allotment papers, and we will look at the record together. I expect to find that the particular—my body, my life—cannot be erased by the universal—the name in the file. I will argue for my right to remain, not by force, but by the logic of the ethical idea itself.\n\nNext week, I intend to explore the markets of Khan Market, where the smell of spices and the sound of bargaining create a symphony of the sensuous. I will also continue my reading of the Phenomenology, finding in my own words a new distance. I am no longer the philosopher who wrote the book, but the man who reads it by the window. I look forward to reporting on the state of the state, and the state of my mind.\n\nG. W. F. Hegel"
